  = help: usage: `data.shapiro_test(var)`

error[Z0401]: `shapiro_test` tests a numeric variable, so `s` must be a Bool, Int or Float variable, but it is a Str
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:41
  |
1 | frame(s = ["a", "b", "c"]).shapiro_test(s)
  |                                         ^ not a numeric variable
  |
  = help: a test of a text or category variable's frequencies is `chi2_test`

error[Z0401]: `shapiro_test` needs at least 3 values of `x`, but only two are present
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:32
  |
1 | frame(x = [1, 2]).shapiro_test(x)
  |                                ^ 2 after dropping NA
  |
  = help: the values are the non-missing rows of the variable; a test of normality says little below 20 or so

note: 2 rows with NA in x dropped (line 1)
error[Z0401]: `shapiro_test` needs at least 3 values of `x`, but only one is present
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:37
  |
1 | frame(x = [1, NA, NA]).shapiro_test(x)
  |                                     ^ 1 after dropping NA
  |
  = help: the values are the non-missing rows of the variable; a test of normality says little below 20 or so

error[Z0401]: every value of `x` is the same, so there is no distribution to test
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:38
  |
1 | frame(x = [2, 2, 2, 2]).shapiro_test(x)
  |                                      ^ one value throughout
  |
  = help: a test needs values that differ between rows

error[Z0421]: `shapiro_test` takes 3 to 5,000 values, but `x` has 5,001
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:38
  |
1 | frame(x = seq(1, 5001)).shapiro_test(x)
  |                                      ^ more than 5,000 values
  |
  = help: Royston's approximation stops at 5,000, as R's `shapiro.test` does; with this many rows, judge normality from `qq_plot`, or test the distribution with `ks_test`

